    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://www.opencastle.dev/og-image.png" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/icon-192.png" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="docs-header">
      <div class="docs-header__inner">
        <div class="docs-header__brand">
          <a href="/" class="docs-header__logo-link">
            <img class="docs-header__icon" src="/icon-192.png" alt="OpenCastle" width="28" height="28" />
          </a>
          <h1 class="docs-header__title">
            <a href="/docs/" class="docs-header__breadcrumb-link">Docs</a>
            <span class="docs-header__breadcrumb-sep">/</span>
            Agent Plugins
          </h1>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="docs-layout">
      <nav class="docs-sidebar" id="docs-sidebar">
        <p class="docs-sidebar__label">Overview</p>
        <ul class="docs-sidebar__list">
          {overviewSections.map((s, i) => (
            <li>
              <a class={`docs-sidebar__link${i === 0 ? ' docs-sidebar__link--active' : ''}`} href={`#${s.id}`}>
                <Fragment set:html={sectionIcons[s.id]} />
                {s.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <div class="docs-sidebar__divider"></div>
        <p class="docs-sidebar__label">Using it</p>
        <ul class="docs-sidebar__list">
          {usingSections.map((s) => (
            <li>
              <a class="docs-sidebar__link" href={`#${s.id}`}>
                <Fragment set:html={sectionIcons[s.id]} />
                {s.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <div class="docs-sidebar__divider"></div>
        <p class="docs-sidebar__label">Reference</p>
        <ul class="docs-sidebar__list">
          {referenceSections.map((s) => (
            <li>
              <a class="docs-sidebar__link" href={`#${s.id}`}>
                <Fragment set:html={sectionIcons[s.id]} />
                {s.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <main class="docs-main">
        <section class="docs-hero">
          <h2 class="docs-hero__heading">Agent Plugins and the open standards</h2>
          <p class="docs-hero__sub">
            The formats AI coding assistants read are converging on open standards. OpenCastle writes
            them wherever an assistant reads them, uses any Agent Plugin in every assistant — including
            the ones that do not read Agent Plugins yet — and publishes your team's standard as one.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ The standards ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="standards">
          <h3 class="section-title">The Standards</h3>
          <p>
            Four open standards now cover most of what an assistant is given. Three are stewarded by the
            Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation; Agent Skills started at Anthropic and is read by
            dozens of tools.
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Covers</th><th>What OpenCastle does with it</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>AGENTS.md</strong></td><td>Project instructions</td><td>Writes it for Codex CLI and OpenCode, and Antigravity reads it beside <code>GEMINI.md</code>; Claude Code gets the same content in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, Copilot, Cursor and Windsurf in their own instruction and rule files</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Agent Skills</strong> (<code>SKILL.md</code>)</td><td>Procedures an agent loads when a task matches</td><td>Every skill — OpenCastle's, your baseline's, your project's — is compiled as a <code>SKILL.md</code> folder named as the skill, where each assistant reads skills</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>MCP</strong></td><td>Tools and data an agent can call</td><td>Writes every server in each assistant's own config, in its own variable syntax — JSON for six, <code>.codex/config.toml</code> for Codex</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Agent Plugins 1.0</strong></td><td>A package of skills and MCP servers</td><td>Uses any Agent Plugin as a baseline, publishes yours as one, and ships each of its {integrationCount} integrations as one</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            What none of them covers yet — custom agents, commands, hooks, scoped rules, a policy for which
            servers may run — OpenCastle still compiles assistant by assistant. Agent Plugins gives that a
            place to live inside a standard package: a client's own namespace.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ The format ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="format">
          <h3 class="section-title">What an Agent Plugin Is</h3>
          <p>
            <a href="https://agent-plugins.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent Plugins 1.0</a>
            was published in August 2026 by maintainers from Amazon, Cursor, Microsoft, OpenAI and Vercel. A
            plugin is a directory:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{pluginTree}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The manifest is closed: ten fields, two required. Skills follow the Agent Skills spec, and a skill
            whose name does not match its directory is skipped. MCP servers are a closed union of three
            transports. Anything a single assistant adds goes under a reverse-domain namespace — OpenCastle's is
            <code>dev.opencastle</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Assistant</th><th>Reads Agent Plugins</th><th>Marketplace file</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>GitHub Copilot (VS Code, CLI)</td><td>Yes, <code>plugin.json</code> at the root</td><td><code>.github/plugin/marketplace.json</code> or <code>.claude-plugin/marketplace.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cursor</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>.cursor-plugin/marketplace.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Codex CLI</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>.agents/plugins/marketplace.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Kiro</td><td>Yes</td><td>—</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td>Its own manifest, <code>.claude-plugin/plugin.json</code>, and <code>.mcp.json</code> — <code>opencastle plugin build</code> writes both</td><td><code>.claude-plugin/marketplace.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Windsurf (Devin Desktop), OpenCode, Antigravity</td><td>Not as plugins — OpenCastle compiles a plugin's skills and servers into their own files</td><td>—</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Extend ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="extend">
          <h3 class="section-title">Use One Everywhere</h3>
          <p>
            Any Agent Plugin can be a baseline. Install it as a devDependency — or keep it in the repository —
            and extend it:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{extendConfig}</code></pre>
          <p>
            <code>opencastle sync</code> then compiles its skills and its servers into every assistant the project compiles for,
            held to your policy and recorded in the lock, like any baseline's. No OpenCastle declaration is
            needed in its <code>package.json</code>: a <code>plugin.json</code> at its root is enough.
          </p>
          <ul class="docs-list">
            <li><strong>Skills</strong> are compiled as they are. One the Agent Skills spec would have an assistant skip — a name that does not match its directory — is reported.</li>
            <li><strong>Servers</strong> in <code>mcp.json</code> become the project's: <code>./</code> and <code>{'${PLUGIN_ROOT}'}</code> become the plugin's path from the project root, where assistants start a project's servers.</li>
            <li>A server that uses <code>{'${PLUGIN_DATA}'}</code>, a working directory of its own, or the legacy SSE transport is left out with the reason: those need an assistant that installs the plugin itself.</li>
            <li>A manifest a conformant client would reject — a missing name, a field of the wrong type — is an error, and nothing is written.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Publish ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="publish">
          <h3 class="section-title">Publish Your Standard</h3>
          <p>
            <code>opencastle baseline init</code> creates a baseline that is also an Agent Plugin. Assistants that
            read Agent Plugins can install its skills and servers as they are; OpenCastle compiles all of it —
            including what the standard does not cover — into every assistant, in every repository that extends it.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{baselineTree}</code></pre>
          <pre><code>{manifest}</code></pre>
          <p>
            A server every assistant can start as it is — a remote server with OAuth, a pinned local one — goes
            in <code>mcp.json</code>. One that needs a token or another value from the developer goes in
            <code>dev.opencastle/config.json</code>, written as <code>{'${NAME}'}</code>: the standard has no
            way to name a secret yet, and OpenCastle spells the variable the way each assistant expects.
          </p>
          <p>
            The scaffold's policy in <code>dev.opencastle/config.json</code> allows only its example servers.
            Add a new server's name to <code>policy.mcp.allow</code>, and a remote server's host to
            <code>policy.mcp.remoteHosts</code>, or <code>opencastle baseline check</code> fails on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Claude Code reads its own manifest, so <code>opencastle plugin build</code> writes
            <code>.claude-plugin/plugin.json</code> and <code>.mcp.json</code> from the portable files, and
            <code>check</code> fails when they fall behind:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{checkOutput}</code></pre>
          <p>
            A skill someone wrote for their own assistant joins the standard with
            <code>opencastle promote skill &lt;name&gt; --to ai-standard</code>, checked against the Agent Skills spec
            and for credentials on the way in. See <a href="/docs/teams#memory">Team Memory</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Marketplace ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="marketplace">
          <h3 class="section-title">Distribute It</h3>
          <p>
            For developers who install plugins in their assistant rather than through a repository, keep your
            plugins in one repository and let <code>opencastle plugin index</code> write the file each assistant
            reads a marketplace from:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{marketTree}</code></pre>
          <pre><code>{indexOutput}</code></pre>
          <pre><code>{marketJson}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Claude Code, Copilot CLI and Cursor read the same shape; Codex reads its own. Each developer adds the
            repository once — <code>claude plugin marketplace add acme/agent-plugins</code>,
            <code>copilot plugin marketplace add acme/agent-plugins</code>,
            <code>codex plugin marketplace add acme/agent-plugins</code>, or in Cursor under Dashboard → Plugins →
            Team Marketplaces → Import. Run <code>opencastle plugin index --check</code> in CI so a new plugin is never
            missing from the list.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Integrations ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="integrations">
          <h3 class="section-title">Integrations</h3>
          <p>
            Each of OpenCastle's {integrationCount} <a href="/docs/plugins">integrations</a> is an Agent Plugin
            directory, checked against the spec in OpenCastle's own tests:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{integrationTree}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Their skills are compiled under the skill's own name — <code>supabase-database</code>, not
            <code>supabase</code> — so an assistant that follows the Agent Skills spec loads them. A team config
            that excludes <code>skills/supabase</code> still works, with a warning naming the new spelling.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Check ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="check">
          <h3 class="section-title">What <code>check</code> Checks</h3>
          <p>
            <code>opencastle plugin check</code> loads a plugin the way the 1.0.0 spec says a conformant client
            must, and reports what such a client would refuse, skip or ignore. <code>baseline check</code> runs the
            same checks on a baseline that is a plugin.
          </p>
          <ul class="docs-list">
            <li><strong>Manifest</strong> — a <code>$schema</code> naming a published version; a name of lowercase letters, digits, hyphens and periods; the types of every field; <code>author</code> holding only name, email and url. An unknown field is reported and ignored, as a client does.</li>
            <li><strong>Skills</strong> — each immediate child of <code>skills/</code> with a <code>SKILL.md</code>: a name matching its directory, a description of at most 1,024 characters, <code>compatibility</code> and <code>metadata</code> in their shapes.</li>
            <li><strong>MCP servers</strong> — a <code>mcp.json</code> on the same version as the manifest; a command that is one executable, bare or <code>./</code>; no <code>PLUGIN_ROOT</code> in <code>env</code>; https unless the host is loopback; no credentials in the URL; no header given twice.</li>
            <li><strong>Containment</strong> — the manifest and every skill resolve inside the plugin, links followed.</li>
            <li><strong>Claude Code's copies</strong> — that <code>.claude-plugin/plugin.json</code> and <code>.mcp.json</code> match the portable files.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Limits ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="limits">
          <h3 class="section-title">Limits</h3>
          <ul class="docs-list">
            <li><strong>Agent Plugins 1.0 covers skills and MCP servers only.</strong> Instructions, agents, prompts, workflows and policy travel in <code>dev.opencastle/</code>, which only OpenCastle reads; an assistant that installs the plugin natively gets its skills and portable servers.</li>
            <li><strong>No secrets in the standard yet.</strong> A portable server that needs a token reads it from the environment the assistant runs in; one that needs a value spelled into its config belongs in <code>dev.opencastle/config.json</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Distribution is not standardised.</strong> Each assistant indexes a marketplace in its own file, which is why <code>plugin index</code> writes three.</li>
            <li><strong>OpenCastle does not fetch plugins.</strong> As with any baseline, your package manager installs and pins it; <code>extends</code> names an installed package or a path.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </main>
    </div>

